ison Chambers, 32 Nassau Street, Dublin D02 YH68\nL115@P6: A CIP catalogue record for this book is available from the British Library\nL116@P6: ISBN 9781529936520\nL117@P6: Penguin Random House is committed to a sustainable future\nL118@P6: for our business, our readers and our planet. This book is made\nL119@P6: from Forest Stewardship Council® certified paper.\nL120@P6-7: Copyrighted MaterialFor Jane\nL121@P7-9: Copyrighted MaterialCopyrighted MaterialPractice even what seems impossible.\nL122@P9: — Marcus Aurelius\nL123@P9-11: Copyrighted MaterialCopyrighted Material CONTENTS\nL124@P11: Introduction xiii\nL125@P11: PART I\nL126@P11: 1. The Bridge 3\nL127@P11: 2. Large-Scale Integration 13\nL128@P11: 3. New Venture 26\nL129@P11: 4. Thirty Days 37\nL130@P11: 5. Going Parallel 45\nL131@P11: 6. Jellyfish 58\nL132@P11: 7. Deathmatch 67\nL133@P11: 8. The Compulsion Loop 81\nL134@P11: 9. CUDA 90\nL135@P11: 10. Resonance 106\nL136@P11-12: 11. AlexNet Copyrighted Material 118PART II\nL137@P12: 12. O.I.A.L.O. 133\nL138@P12: 13. Superintelligence 144\nL139@P12: 14. The Good Year 156\nL140@P12: 15. The Transformer 170\nL141@P12: 16. Hyperscale 181\nL142@P12: 17. Money 189\nL143@P12: 18. Spaceships 197\nL144@P12: 19. Power 204\nL145@P12: 20. The Most Important Stock on Earth 210\nL146@P12: 21. Jensen 222\nL147@P12: 22. The Fear 230\nL148@P12: 23. The Thinking Machine 241\nL149@P12: Acknowledgments 247\nL150@P12: About the Author 249\nL151@P12-13: Copyrighted MaterialINTRODUCTION\nL152@P13: T\nL153@P13: his is the story of how a niche vendor of video game hardware became\nL154@P13: the most valuable company in the world. It is the story of a stubborn\nL155@P13: entrepreneur who pushed his radical vision for computing for thirty\nL156@P13: years, in the process becoming one of the wealthiest men alive. It is the story\nL157@P13: of a revolution in silicon and the small group of renegade engineers who de\u0002fied Wall Street to make it happen. And it is the story of the birth of an awe\u0002some and terrifying new category of artificial intelligence, whose long- term\nL158@P13: implications for the human species cannot be known.\nL159@P13: At the center of this story is a propulsive, mercurial, brilliant, and extraor\u0002dinarily dedicated man. His name is Jensen Huang, and his thirty- two- year\nL160@P13: tenure is the longest of any technology CEO in the S& P 500. Huang is a vi\u0002sionary inventor whose familiarity with the inner workings of electronic cir\u0002cuitry approaches a kind of intimacy. He reasons from first principles about\nL161@P13: what microchips can do today, then gambles with great conviction on what\nL162@P13: they will do tomorrow. He does not always win, but when he does, he wins\nL163@P13: big: his early, all- in bet on AI was one of the best investments in Silicon Val\u0002ley history. Huang’s company, Nvidia, is today worth more than $3 trillion,\nL164@P13: rivaling both Apple and Microsoft in value.\nL165@P13-14: Copyrighted Materialxiv introduction\nL166@P14: In person, Huang is charming, funny, self- deprecating, and frequently\nL167@P14:  self- contradictory. He keeps up a semicomic deadpan patter at all times. We\nL168@P14: met in 2023 for breakfast at a Denny’s diner, his favorite restaurant chain.\nL169@P14: Huang had developed the business plan for Nvidia at this same restaurant\nL170@P14: thirty years earlier; chatting with our waitress, he ordered seven items, in\u0002cluding a Super Bird sandwich and a chicken- fried steak. “You know, I\nL171@P14: used to be a dishwasher here,” he told her. “But I worked hard! Like, really\nL172@P14: hard. So I got to be a busboy.”\nL173@P14: Huang, born in Taiwan, immigrated to the United States when he was\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe man from the future : the visionary life of John von Neumann  - Free Library Catalog (https://catalog.freelibrary.org/Record/2593454)\nciteturn59view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawl