NCLUSION The Future of Network Effects ������������������������������371\nL92@P6: ACKNOWLEDGMENTS�������������������������������������������������������������� 375\nL93@P6: NOTES������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� 379\nL94@P6-8: ColdStartProb_9780062969743_AS0928_cc20_Final.indd 7 9/28/21 4:42 PMColdStartProb_9780062969743_AS0928_cc20_Final.indd 8 9/28/21 4:42 PMINTRODUCTION\nL95@P8: I\nL96@P8: t was a Friday evening in December 2015, and the office was buzzing.\nL97@P8: Amid the vast, monochromatic corridors of Uber’s San Francisco\nL98@P8: headquarters at 1455 Market Street—two football fields’ worth of\nL99@P8: gleaming LED lights, light woods, concrete, and steel—the office was\nL100@P8: still mostly occupied at 8 p.m. Some sat at their desks quietly typing\nL101@P8: emails while others debated energetically with colleagues over video\u0002conference. Others were drawing on whiteboards, hosting impromptu\nL102@P8: jam sessions to tackle the tricky operational problems facing who\u0002knows-what. And a few pairs of employees were walking up and down\nL103@P8: the main flow in one-on-one meetings, some in intense discussion and\nL104@P8: others just catching up.\nL105@P8: Everywhere you looked, there were reminders of the global scale of\nL106@P8: Uber’s business as well as the international heritage of the team driving\nL107@P8: it. Colorful flags from every country hung from the ceiling. Conference\nL108@P8: room screens hosted videoconferences with colleagues from faraway\nL109@P8: offices in Jakarta, São Paulo, and Dubai—sometimes simultaneously!\nL110@P8: Flat-screen TVs were scattered throughout the floor showing metrics,\nL111@P8: broken down by mega-region, country, and city, so that teams could\nL112@P8: monitor progress. The global culture seeped into the naming conven\u0002tions for conference rooms: near the entrance, the names started with\nL113@P8: Abu Dhabi and Amsterdam, and at the far other end of the floor, they\nL114@P8: ended with Vienna, Washington, and Zurich.\nL115@P8: At first glance Uber might just look like a simple app—after all,\nL116@P8-9: ColdStartProb_9780062969743_AS0928_cc20_Final.indd 1 9/28/21 4:42 PM2 THE COLD START PROBLEM\nL117@P9: the premise was always to hit a button and get a ride. But underneath\nL118@P9: its deceptively basic user interface was a complex, global operation re\u0002quired to sustain the business. The app sat on a vast worldwide network\nL119@P9: of smaller networks, each one representing cities and countries. Each of\nL120@P9: these networks had to be started, scaled, and defended against compet\u0002itors, at all hours of the day.\nL121@P9: It was in my role at Uber that I really came to viscerally understand\nL122@P9: networks, supply and demand, network effects, and their immense\nL8636@P386: 5. M. Kathryn Davis, “Sardine oil on troubled waters: The boom and\nL8637@P386: bust of California’s sardine industry 1905–1955,” University of Cali\u0002fornia, Berkeley, 2002.\nL8638@P386: 6. Naval Ravikant, Twitter post, June 2017, https://twitter.com/naval\nL8639@P386: /status/877467713811042304?lang=en.\nL8640@P386: 7. Reid Hoffman, “The Big Pivot,” Masters of Scale, Podcast audio, July\nL8641@P386: 2019, https://mastersofscale.com/stewart-butterfield-the-big-pivot/.\nL8642@P386: 8. Stewart Butterfield, interview with the author over videoconference,\nL8643@P386: April 2020.\nL8644@P386-387: ColdStartProb_9780062969743_AS0928_cc20_Final.indd 379 9/28/21 4:42 PM380 NOTES\nL8645@P387: 9. First Round Review, “From 0 to $1B—Slack’s Founder Shares Their\nL8646@P387: Epic Launch Strategy,” February 2015, https://review.firstround\nL8647@P387: .com/From-0-to-1B-Slacks-Founder-Shares-Their-Epic-Launch\nL8648@P387: -Strategy.\nL8649@P387: 10. Eric Yuan, interview with the author, San Jose, February 2020.\nL8650@P387: 11. Jonathan Golden, “Lessons Learned Scaling Airbnb 100X,” Me\u0002dium, August 2017, https://medium.com/@jgolden/