nter 111\nL140@P7: CHAPTER 5: Hook’s Captain 145\nL141@P7: CHAPTER 6: \u001f e Empire of Wind and Light 188\nL142@P7: NOTES 215\nL143@P7: ACKNOWLEDGMENTS 265\nL144@P7: INDEX 269\nL145@P7-9: Copyrighted MaterialCopyrighted MaterialINTRODUCTION\nL146@P9: All Roads Lead to Rome\nL147@P9: I\nL148@P9: t’s easy to descend into the underground empire. \u001f ere are entrances\nL149@P9: everywhere. Some of them are even signposted. In the Washington,\nL150@P9: DC, area, where both of us live, the multilane artery of Route I- 66\nL151@P9: connects the Virginia suburbs to the capital city of the United States.\nL152@P9: Smaller roads diverge from it toward the Pentagon and the CIA’s head\u0002quarters in Langley. Route I- 66 passes through the Beltway, which\nL153@P9: loops around the city toward Fort Meade, where the spies and hackers\nL154@P9: of the National Security Agency (NSA) and U.S. Cyber Command go\nL155@P9: to work. \u001f e road terminates in Foggy Bottom, the home of the U.S.\nL156@P9: Department of State, a few blocks away from the U.S. Department of\nL157@P9: the Treasury and the White House.\nL158@P9: \u001f ese buildings are the surface manifestations of American\nL159@P9: empire. Some were designed for public display. \u001f e White House and\nL160@P9-10: Department of the Treasury have Palladian facades constructed accord\u0002ing to principles descending from the Roman architect Vitruvius, who Copyrighted Material2 UNDERGROUND EMPIRE\nL161@P10: began as an engineer in Julius Caesar’s army. Others are built from\nL162@P10: reinforced steel and concrete for more utilitarian purposes, barricaded\nL163@P10: behind fences, cameras, and armed guards.\nL164@P10: All are connected to the underworld. Every empire’s architecture\nL165@P10: of rule and display would fall into ruin were it not joined to a\nL166@P10:  ligree of\nL167@P10: tunnels and pipes, conveying resources and information like the myce\u0002lium that spreads out from a mushroom through the surrounding soil.\nL168@P10: \u001f e threads of empire operate in both directions, spreading in\nL169@P10:  uence\nL170@P10: and power outward as they gather resources to the center.\nL171@P10: \u001f e rulers of the ancient world built their capitols from stones\nL172@P10: like porphyry and marble. \u001f eir living empires were made out of more\nL173@P10: everyday stu\nL174@P10:  ; the trade routes, grain ships, and aqueducts that tied\nL175@P10: towns, cities, and countryside in a densely connected web of economic\nL176@P10: activity. Imperial Rome built a network of roads that allowed mer\u0002chants to convey their wares as well as legions to march quickly across\nL177@P10: provinces. When a traveler entered the empire from the hinterlands,\n"
